const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Original Research Article Writing Service","serviceType":"Research paper writing, manuscript development and journal submission support","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/original-research-article/","description":"Original research articles written from your data to the reporting guideline your study needs, with 365 days of revision support.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"How can I structure an original research article for journal submission?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In IMRaD — introduction, methods, results and discussion — with a structured abstract, keywords, references, tables and figures around it, and all of it to the journal’s own headings rather than generic ones. But the structure is the easy half. What actually decides whether the paper can be judged is the reporting guideline your design falls under, because that determines which specific items each section is responsible for: CONSORT for a randomised trial, STROBE for cohort, case-control and cross-sectional studies, STARD for diagnostic accuracy, PRISMA for a systematic review, CARE for a case report, ARRIVE for animal work, TREND for non-randomised intervention evaluations. Take the current checklist from the EQUATOR Network, write to it from the outline stage rather than checking against it at the end, and complete it honestly — the gaps it exposes are exactly the ones a reviewer will find."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What are best practices for writing a clear and precise methodology section?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Write it so somebody else could repeat the study, which is a considerably higher bar than describing what you did. In order: the design named explicitly rather than left to be inferred; the setting, the dates and how participants were identified; eligibility criteria as they were actually applied; every variable defined, including the confounders and how they were chosen; the measurement methods and their comparability across groups; how the sample size was arrived at; the statistical methods with the model, the handling of missing data and which analyses were pre-specified; and the ethics approval with its reference number. Two things carry more weight than their length suggests. For a trial, allocation concealment and who was blinded — two lines that a methods reviewer looks for first. For an observational study, how the confounder set was selected: an adjusted estimate with an unexplained adjustment set is the commonest reason such a paper is sent back."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I present statistical results effectively in my research article?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lead with the effect size and its confidence interval, and treat the p-value as secondary. An interval tells a reader the range of effects your data is compatible with; a p-value on its own does not, and it is why “no significant difference” is so often misleading — a hazard ratio of 0.91 with an interval from 0.72 to 1.15 has not excluded a clinically important benefit or harm, and saying it found no difference misreports it. Report every outcome you said you would, including the ones that did not go your way. Give counts alongside percentages so a reader can recompute. Say which analyses were pre-specified and which were exploratory. For subgroups, report the interaction test rather than a p-value within each subgroup, because a difference between strata is what you are claiming and the within-stratum p-value is not evidence of it. And keep interpretation out of the results entirely — that is what the discussion is for, and a reviewer will say so."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How can I write a compelling discussion and conclusion section?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Four moves, in order. State the key result in relation to the objective, once, without restating the results section. Put it alongside what is already known and say specifically where it agrees and disagrees rather than listing adjacent studies. Then the limitations — and this is where most discussions lose the reviewer, because a generic paragraph about sample size and single-centre design reads as a formality. Name your actual weaknesses, and for each one say which direction it would push the estimate. A reviewer who sees that you have understood your own study’s vulnerabilities reads everything else more generously. Finally the conclusion, proportionate to the design: an observational study supports the case for a trial, not a change in practice. That last sentence is the one authors resist most, and it is the one that stops a reviewer deciding you have over-read your own data."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I ensure my article meets journal formatting and citation requirements?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Read the author guidelines for the specific journal, not the publisher’s general page, and read them before writing rather than after. What varies and matters: the word limit and whether it includes the abstract and references; the abstract’s required headings, which are journal-specific and using the wrong ones is a common desk rejection; the citation style, which in medicine is usually Vancouver as set out in the NLM and ICMJE recommendations, though AMA and APA appear too; limits on the number of tables, figures and references; figure format and resolution; and which declarations and statements are mandatory. Use a reference manager rather than formatting by hand, but check the output against the sources, because a manager reproduces whatever is in the record including its errors. Then complete the reporting checklist with page numbers, which most journals now ask for as a submitted item."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What common mistakes should I avoid when preparing a research manuscript?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Eight, in roughly the order we find them. Causal language for an observational design — “reduces”, “improves”, “demonstrates” — which is the single most common problem and the most fixable. Methods too thin to judge, usually because they lost the word-limit fight to the introduction. Subgroup findings reported without an interaction test. A null result described as showing no difference, when the confidence interval says otherwise. Outcomes reported that differ from the ones registered or protocolled, which registries now make visible and reviewers now check. Conclusions that outrun the design. An introduction of four paragraphs where two would do. And the annexes left to the end: the ethics reference, the registration number, the declarations and the completed checklist, which is where submissions fail on the final afternoon. Six of the eight are decided before drafting starts."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How can I respond effectively to peer review comments?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Triage every comment into one of three: concede and change; answer with evidence and change nothing; or decline with a reason. Most authors do the first two and avoid the third, which is a mistake — a reviewer who is wrong about something can be answered courteously, and editors expect it. Then write the response as a point-by-point letter, reproducing each comment and answering it directly underneath, with the page and line of every change so nobody has to hunt. Make the changes visible with tracked changes as well. Tone matters more than authors expect: reviewers are unpaid, they read your paper closely enough to find the problem, and a response that reads as irritated affects the editor’s decision. And answer every comment, including the ones you think are minor. An unanswered comment is the thing a reviewer notices on the second round."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How can professional editing or support improve my research manuscript?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Mostly by reading it as a reviewer will rather than as its author does. The three things that change outcomes are structural rather than linguistic: checking every claim against what the design supports, which is where most sound studies lose credibility; working through the reporting checklist item by item to find the missing lines; and protecting the methods budget when the word limit bites. Language editing sits underneath all of that and matters for a different reason — a paper that is hard to read is read less carefully, and a reviewer who has to work to follow the argument attributes the difficulty to the science. What professional support does not do is improve the study. If the design cannot support the claim, better writing makes that clearer rather than less clear, and we would rather say so at the quote stage than at review."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which reporting guideline applies to my study, and where do I find it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The design decides it. Randomised trial: CONSORT 2025, with the extension matching your design — cluster, crossover, stepped-wedge, non-inferiority, pilot, N-of-1, patient-reported outcomes, harms, or AI-enabled intervention. Cohort, case-control or cross-sectional: STROBE, with RECORD if the data was routinely collected and STROBE-MR for Mendelian randomisation. Diagnostic or prognostic accuracy: STARD, with TRIPOD+AI where the index test is a model. Systematic review: PRISMA 2020, with PRISMA-S for the search and PRISMA-ScR for scoping reviews. Case report: CARE. Animal study: ARRIVE 2.0. Non-randomised evaluation of a behavioural or public health intervention: TREND. Qualitative research: COREQ or SRQR. The EQUATOR Network maintains all of them with their current versions, and current is the operative word — CONSORT was revised in 2025 and PRISMA in 2020, and writing to a superseded version is a visible error."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you turn my thesis chapter into a journal article?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and it is a rewrite rather than a trim, which is the part that surprises people. A thesis chapter is written to demonstrate competence to an examiner: the literature review is exhaustive because breadth is being assessed, the methods narrate what you learned as you went, and the discussion covers everything. A paper is written to convince a reviewer of one claim in three and a half thousand words, so the literature review becomes two paragraphs that establish a gap, the methods become a reproducible account with the false starts removed, and the discussion argues a single point. It is also common that one chapter contains two papers, or that two chapters make one — deciding that is the first conversation. Two practical points: check your university’s policy on publishing from a deposited thesis, since some repositories make the chapter publicly available and a journal may treat that as prior publication; and settle authorship with your supervisor in writing before drafting rather than after."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How long should each section be?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"As a working proportion for a 3,500-word original article: introduction around 500 words, methods 800 to 1,000, results 800 to 1,000, discussion 900 to 1,200. The introduction is the section most often twice the length it should be, and the methods the section most often half. A useful discipline is that the introduction has three jobs and about four paragraphs in which to do them — what is known, what is not, and what this study did — and that anything else belongs in the discussion. The submission timeline planner and the manuscript skeleton on this page both work from the journal’s own limit rather than a generic one."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you help choose the journal as well?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and it is better done before the final draft than after. Journals differ in what they will consider, not only in what they will accept: a single-centre observational study, a null result, a study without a pre-registered protocol, or a device paper without a comparator each have a narrower set of realistic homes than the field as a whole. Deciding early means the paper is written to a real target — its structure, its word limit, its reporting checklist — rather than reformatted twice after the first two rejections. Our journal selection service does this from the manuscript rather than from a list."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What do we do about an author who will not respond?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Deal with it before submission, because it becomes considerably harder afterwards. ICMJE requires every listed author to approve the final version and to agree to be accountable for it, and most journals now ask the corresponding author to confirm that in writing. A silent co-author is therefore a submission blocker rather than an inconvenience. In practice the routes are: a documented deadline with a clear statement of what will happen, a move to the acknowledgements if their contribution does not meet the authorship criteria, and — if it does and they cannot be reached — a conversation with the editor rather than a submission that misrepresents approval. Settling authorship in writing at the start prevents nearly all of this."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do we handle a result that contradicts our hypothesis?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Report it, in the section where results belong, with the same detail as a confirmatory one. A study that pre-specified an outcome and found no effect is a finding, and it is increasingly publishable — registered reports exist precisely to remove the incentive to bury it. What damages a paper is not the null result but the response to it: an outcome quietly swapped for one that worked, a subgroup promoted to the abstract, or a discussion that argues the study was underpowered without saying so in the limitations. The discussion should state what the confidence interval rules out, which is usually a more useful sentence than any claim the data could have supported."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Our study is small and single-centre. Is it publishable?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Frequently, provided the paper claims what the design supports. A small single-centre study cannot establish generalisable effectiveness, and a discussion that implies it will draw the objection that sinks the paper. What it can do well is report a precise, well-characterised population, describe an effect with its interval, and say what a larger study would need to test. Journals that publish this kind of work exist and are identifiable in advance. The two things that decide the outcome are whether the reporting is complete against the relevant checklist and whether the limitations section names the real limitations rather than the conventional ones."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Should my manuscript be in British or American English?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Follow the journal, and then be consistent, because a mixed manuscript is one of the first things a copy-editor flags. The pairs that matter most in research writing are randomisation and randomization, analyse and analyze, modelling and modeling, labelling and labeling, behaviour and behavior, centre and center, and programme and program — and most American journals want randomized controlled trial rather than randomised. Two traps a spell-checker will not catch. Oxford style uses -ize endings in British English, so organize and summarize are correct British spellings in some journals and wrong in others. And the spelling of a proper noun does not change: the World Health Organization keeps its z whichever variety you are writing in. We set the variety at the start from the target journal and apply it to the whole manuscript, including the figure labels and the supplementary files, which is where inconsistency usually survives."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Physician writing","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Original research article","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/original-research-article/"}]}];

